y threshold collision, the recusal trigger, and deadlock resolution, so I'll ask for concrete wording on each. The Steward's own acknowledgment of the proposal is the last thing I still need to look at.\n\nNext I'm weighing whether the Council can amend the proposal during deliberation, citing earlier precedents where it did. Since this adds several new provisions, including the floor, recusal trigger, and deadlock resolution, I'm wondering whether that much drafting can realistically happen in a sitting. My options are voting yes with amendments, voting no, or deferring for revised text, and I'm leaning toward supporting the principle while flagging the current gaps.\n\nI'm now weighing whether the proposal's wording can realistically be fixed in this sitting. Its text is very sparse, with no floor and no mechanism for the recusal trigger, so I'm considering whether to draft a complete amendment and vote on that, or defer until a full text is ready.\n\nI'm settling on a position for Round 1: I support the intent, but I won't vote yes on the text as drafted. I'll state that I'll vote yes only if the amendment covers the four gaps, so the preliminary vote is conditional.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote \"no\" because the text is incomplete or \"yes\" with conditions, and I'm leaning toward a preliminary yes that signals support for the direction while making the final vote depend on amending the text. Since a constitutional vote needs unanimity, a no would fail the proposal outright, so I'm checking that the conditional yes conveys the right position.\n\nI'm settling on a preliminary yes, but only with conditions that the final text has to meet. Since the Steward has committed to voting yes, all four of us would have to agree, so a single no would sink it.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LjQj42mPSRM4WiifFN4UbL","name":"ask_clerk","input":{"comment_numbers":[42,47]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequ